# deploy/.env.example — copy to deploy/.env (chmod 600) and fill in.
#
# This is the first .env.example anywhere in this repo scoped to the Rust
# binaries (#139 — the root .env.example is admin-console-scoped only).
# Every var below traces to a real `#[arg(long, env = "...")]` in
# backends/apps/sms-gateway/src/main.rs or backends/apps/sms-worker/src/main.rs, or to a
# service block in docker-compose.yml — nothing here is speculative.
#
# `docker compose --env-file .env up -d --build` reads this automatically
# if it's named `.env` in this directory (Compose's own default); this
# file is the template. Never commit the real `.env` — see
# docs/runbooks/deployment.adoc's "Secrets" section for the threat model
# this simple approach accepts and what it doesn't protect against.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Image tag/owner (S6/owner-knob) — optional. Every service that has a
# published GHCR image carries both `build:` (source, the default —
# leave these unset and `docker compose up -d --build` never reads them)
# and `image:`, so `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d` (no
# `--build`) is also a real, working upgrade path once a tagged release
# exists — see docs/runbooks/deployment.adoc's "Releasing a new version"
# section.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# docker-compose.yml's own default is ghcr.io/vaam-apps/... at
# VSMS_IMAGE_TAG v0.3.1 — this repository's GitHub owner has moved twice:
# v0.2.1 and earlier published under ghcr.io/vymalo/...; v0.3.0 alone
# published under ghcr.io/vaam-store/...; v0.3.1 and every tag after it
# lands under ghcr.io/vaam-apps/..., this file's own default now.
# .github/workflows/release.yml publishes to
# ghcr.io/${{ github.repository_owner }}/... unconditionally — pulling an
# older release means setting BOTH knobs together, never one without the
# other:
# VSMS_IMAGE_OWNER=vymalo
# VSMS_IMAGE_TAG=v0.2.1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_USER=vsms
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=vsms

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# sms-gateway and sms-worker (shared)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OAuth2 client_credentials credentials for Orange Cameroon's SMS API.
# sms-gateway needs *at least one* provider configured (the DLR route has
# to have something to parse callbacks against) — Orange, MTN or AWS (see
# AWS_SMS_EVENTS_TOPIC_ARN below), any one satisfies it. sms-worker needs
# one only when SMS_WORKER_ROLES includes `dispatch`. All three Orange
# values are all-or-none, and Orange is optional: an AWS-only or MTN-only
# deployment comments this whole block out.
#
# ****  FILL ALL THREE, OR COMMENT ALL THREE OUT; NEVER LEAVE THEM BLANK.  ****
#
# Same trap as the MTN and AWS blocks below: docker-compose.yml passes these
# through only when set, so a blank `ORANGE_CM_CLIENT_ID=` here is an empty
# string to the binary, which reads as Some("") and aborts startup. They
# used to be `${...:?}` required; an Orange deployment must now uncomment
# and fill them.
#ORANGE_CM_CLIENT_ID=
#ORANGE_CM_CLIENT_SECRET=
# E.164, no `tel:` scheme, e.g. +2370000
#ORANGE_CM_SENDER_NUMBER=
# Real Orange sandbox/production host — override only to point at a
# non-default environment.
ORANGE_CM_BASE_URL=https://api.orange.com

# MTN Cameroon via MADAPI. Entirely optional — an Orange-only deployment
# leaves this whole block commented out.
#
# ****  COMMENT THESE OUT; DO NOT LEAVE THEM BLANK.  ****
#
# That is not style. An env var that is present-but-empty is a *value* to
# clap, not an absence, and MTN_TPS_CEILING= (empty) aborts sms-gateway at
# startup before any of this repo's own validation runs:
#
#   error: invalid value '' for '--mtn-tps-ceiling <MTN_TPS_CEILING>':
#          cannot parse float from empty string
#
# An empty MTN_CLIENT_ID= is subtler and just as fatal: it reads as
# Some("") and trips the all-or-none check with a message about flags you
# never set. Docker Compose passes `FOO=` through as an empty value, so a
# half-filled .env is a crash-looping gateway, not a disabled provider.
#
# The five below are all-or-none; MTN_SENDER_ID is optional on top.
#MTN_CLIENT_ID=
#MTN_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The approved short code, e.g. 11221
#MTN_SERVICE_CODE=
# Commercial terms of your specific MTN contract — absent from MADAPI's
# own Swagger, so there is deliberately no default. Inventing a TPS
# ceiling either throttles a paid contract or gets the account rate
# limited; inventing a price misprices estimatedCostXaf.
#MTN_TPS_CEILING=5.0
#MTN_COST_PER_SEGMENT_XAF=16.00
# Optional. MADAPI's senderAddress wins over the service code when set;
# a per-message sender ID from sendMessage wins over both.
#MTN_SENDER_ID=
#MTN_SUPPORTS_ALPHANUMERIC_SENDER=false
#MTN_BASE_URL=https://api.mtn.com
#
# Setting these is not sufficient for delivery receipts. MADAPI sends none
# until a deliveryReportUrl is registered against your service code:
#   sms-gateway mtn-subscribe-dlr --delivery-report-url https://<host>/dlr/mtn_cm
# See docs/runbooks/deployment.adoc's "Enabling MTN delivery receipts".

# AWS End User Messaging SMS (provider key `aws_sms`). Entirely optional —
# deployments without it leave this whole block commented out.
#
# ****  COMMENT THESE OUT; DO NOT LEAVE THEM BLANK.  ****
#
# Same reason as the MTN block above, and just as fatal: an env var that is
# present-but-empty is a *value* to clap, not an absence.
# AWS_SMS_TPS_CEILING= (empty) aborts sms-worker with `cannot parse float
# from empty string`, and an empty AWS_SMS_REGION= reads as Some("") and
# trips the all-or-none check. Docker Compose passes `FOO=` through as an
# empty value.
#
# Two halves, read by two different binaries:
#
# SENDING — read by sms-worker only (the `dispatch` role). The first six
# are all-or-none; the rest are optional.
#AWS_SMS_REGION=eu-west-1
# Long-lived IAM user keys only (no session tokens / instance profiles).
#AWS_SMS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# SECRET — keep it out of version control and logs, exactly like
# ORANGE_CM_CLIENT_SECRET.
#AWS_SMS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# The configuration set whose event destination publishes to the SNS topic
# below. Required: without one AWS reports nothing.
#AWS_SMS_CONFIGURATION_SET=
# Commercial terms of your AWS account — there is deliberately no default.
# An invented TPS ceiling throttles you or gets the account rate-limited; an
# invented price misprices estimatedCostXaf. AWS prices in USD per
# destination country: the cost is that price converted to XAF.
#AWS_SMS_TPS_CEILING=
#AWS_SMS_COST_PER_SEGMENT_XAF=
# Optional. A pool / phone number / sender ID in the AWS account, used when
# no approved per-message sender ID is passed.
#AWS_SMS_ORIGINATION_IDENTITY=
# Optional. `transactional` (default) or `promotional`.
#AWS_SMS_MESSAGE_TYPE=transactional
#AWS_SMS_SUPPORTS_ALPHANUMERIC_SENDER=false
# Optional. Overrides https://sms-voice.<region>.amazonaws.com/ (VPC
# endpoints, a local emulator).
#AWS_SMS_ENDPOINT_URL=
# Optional. Seconds AWS keeps trying to deliver one message (5..=259200).
# Unset, AWS tries for up to 72 hours, so an OTP vsms already expired
# (15 minutes by default) can still arrive days later. Match the shortest
# validity of the traffic routed to AWS: 900 for an OTP-only route. Other
# classes default to a 24 hour validity, which 900 would cut short.
#AWS_SMS_TIME_TO_LIVE_SECONDS=900
#
# RECEIVING — read by sms-gateway only, and the ONLY AWS variable it reads:
# it needs no AWS credentials, because it authenticates deliveries by
# verifying Amazon SNS message signatures against this topic. The ARN of the
# SNS topic the configuration set publishes to. Setting it mounts
# POST /dlr/aws_sms; subscribe the topic to https://<host>/dlr/aws_sms.
# Without it messages submit, reach `submitted`, and sit there.
#AWS_SMS_EVENTS_TOPIC_ARN=arn:aws:sns:eu-west-1:123456789012:vsms-sms-events
#
# Also seed the provider (this stack has no seed one-shot; see the comment
# on sms-gateway in docker-compose.yml). For an AWS-ONLY deployment do NOT
# run the bare `seed-dispatch` or `bootstrap` — they seed `orange_cm`.

# sms-gateway only (#134/#140). The server-held pepper behind
# Message.msisdnHash/bodyHash (HMAC-SHA256, sms_api::pepper) — real secret
# material, minimum 32 bytes, validated at startup: a missing or
# too-short value fails sms-gateway's own boot, before it ever binds a
# listener. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Rotating this later does not retroactively rehash already-stored rows —
# see sms_api::pepper's own module doc for the consequence before rotating
# against a database with real traffic in it.
SMS_HASH_PEPPER=

# Comma-separated, no spaces: any of dispatch,drain,scheduler,hooks,jobs
# (smpp is scaffold-only, milestone 7). §9.2's own deployment diagram runs
# a second, scale-to-N-only worker node (--roles hooks,jobs) once load
# calls for it — see docs/runbooks/deployment.adoc for that as a day-2 step,
# not something this compose file stands up by default.
SMS_WORKER_ROLES=dispatch,drain,scheduler,hooks,jobs

# Optional: RUST_LOG-style filters, passed through docker-compose.yml as
# SMS_GATEWAY_LOG / SMS_WORKER_LOG. Leave unset for each binary's own
# sensible default.
# SMS_GATEWAY_LOG=sms_gateway=info,sms_api=info,sms_provider_aws_sms=info,cratestack=info
# SMS_WORKER_LOG=sms_worker=info,sms_worker_bin=info

# Optional (#153): sms-gateway only. IdempotencyLayer/RateLimitLayer
# tuning — see docs/architecture.md §4.5/§4.6. Leave unset for the
# defaults below (matching that section's own figures, already verified
# against this workspace's real live-suite call volume).
# SMS_IDEMPOTENCY_TTL_SECS=86400
# SMS_RATE_LIMIT_BURST=120
# SMS_RATE_LIMIT_REFILL_PER_SECOND=2.0

# Optional (#163): sms-gateway only. The second, coarser RateLimitLayer on
# the authenticated API surface — keyed on the real TCP peer, not the
# verified principal SMS_RATE_LIMIT_BURST above buckets by, so a flood of
# forged requests cannot buy itself a fresh bucket. See
# sms_api::default_source_rate_limit_config's own doc for why this budget
# is sized differently (wider) than SMS_RATE_LIMIT_BURST — it covers every
# client sharing this deployment's Caddy/admin ingress at once, not one.
# SMS_SOURCE_RATE_LIMIT_BURST=1200
# SMS_SOURCE_RATE_LIMIT_REFILL_PER_SECOND=10.0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# backup (#69) — see docs/runbooks/backup-restore.adoc for the full decision
# and the restore drill. Nightly pg_dump (custom format), not WAL
# archiving/PITR: a single-VM deployment with no live traffic yet doesn't
# carry the always-on WAL-shipping pipeline's cost, and the upgrade path
# is documented rather than silently foreclosed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the dump + manifest land. Provider-agnostic (rclone): an
# S3-compatible bucket, Backblaze B2, GCS, Azure Blob, or a bare local
# path (drills only — same-disk backups protect against very little, see
# the runbook). No default: this service refuses to start rather than
# back up to nowhere.
#   e.g. s3:my-bucket/vsms-backups
BACKUP_RCLONE_REMOTE=

# How long backups are kept in BACKUP_RCLONE_REMOTE before rclone prunes
# them on the next run.
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Standard 5-field cron syntax, evaluated in the backup container's own
# UTC clock by `vsms-backup schedule` (deploy/backup-tool) — an in-process
# scheduler, not Alpine's busybox crond any more (containerize-tooling
# PR); a 6/7-field seconds-first expression also works, unchanged.
BACKUP_CRON_SCHEDULE=0 3 * * *

# Take one backup immediately on container start, in addition to the cron
# schedule above — so a fresh deploy has a restorable backup right away
# rather than waiting up to 24h for the first scheduled tick.
BACKUP_RUN_ON_START=true

# Where `vsms-backup schedule` touches a file after every *successful*
# backup, and where `vsms-backup healthcheck` (this image's own
# HEALTHCHECK) reads it back from — see deploy/backup-tool's own
# schedule.rs module doc. Not a tuning value most deployments need to
# change; overridable mainly so a test can point it somewhere throwaway.
# BACKUP_HEALTH_FILE=/tmp/vsms-backup-last-success

# The remote's own credentials go in deploy/secrets/rclone.conf (rclone's
# own config format — `rclone config` writes it interactively, or write
# it by hand: https://rclone.org/docs/#config-config-file), gitignored
# and mounted read-only, same convention as
# deploy/secrets/console-composer.pem. Not needed at all when
# BACKUP_RCLONE_REMOTE is a bare local path.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# admin console
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The console has no login: no password, no session, no OIDC client. A browser
# pairs by making a key it cannot export, and a vaam admin phone (or, as the
# break-glass, `sms-gateway pair-console-key`) vouches for it. The server only
# carries sealed bytes, so what it is configured with is where the gateway is
# (SMS_API_URL, set by docker-compose.yml: the gateway's ORIGIN, with no path
# prefix, because every request path replaces a base path and the console
# refuses to boot on one), the gateway's two PUBLIC sealing keys and the
# composer's service key. docs/design/console-pairing.md.
#
# `sms-gateway server-keys` prints both keys (`current:` and `next:`). They are
# public, and they must be the pair the gateway signs with (the files in
# deploy/secrets/envelope-*.pem): the browser believes no answer sealed under
# anything else.
SMS_ENVELOPE_SERVER_KEY_CURRENT=
SMS_ENVELOPE_SERVER_KEY_NEXT=

# The composer sends through a service key of its own, an Ed25519 AppClient
# key that is NOT any person's: deploy/secrets/console-composer.pem, owned by
# admin's runtime uid (10001) at mode 0400. Generate it and register its
# public half with `sms-gateway register-client --client-name
# console-composer` (docs/runbooks/deployment.adoc, "The composer's key").

MESSAGE_STREAM_POLL_MS=2000
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME=vsms Admin Console

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Caddy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Real deploy: point real DNS A/AAAA records at this VM for both, and
# Caddy's automatic HTTPS (ACME) needs nothing further. Local/demo run
# with no public DNS: see deploy/Caddyfile's own comment for the
# `tls internal` swap, and use any hostname you can resolve locally
# (e.g. via /etc/hosts) — it doesn't need to be a real domain in that mode.
SMS_GATEWAY_DOMAIN=api.example.com
SMS_ADMIN_DOMAIN=admin.example.com
CADDY_ACME_EMAIL=ops@example.com

# Host ports Caddy publishes. Not 80/443 by default — this machine may
# already have something bound there. Set both to the standard ports for
# a real deploy.
CADDY_HTTP_PORT=8080
CADDY_HTTPS_PORT=8443

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Caddy rate limiting (#156)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `/dlr/{providerKey}` is unauthenticated at the edge by design
# (docs/architecture.md §4.6; #34's own module doc for the DLR route) and
# does a Postgres round trip on every call, so it is rate limited at this
# Caddy edge — see deploy/Caddyfile's own comment for the reasoning. (The
# `/token` zones are gone with the token endpoint, VS-4.) Every value below
# is a bucket's *capacity*, not its *key*; retuning these does not change
# what a bucket is keyed by.
#
# All four are optional — every one has the default deploy/Caddyfile
# already falls back to if unset, shown here. Uncomment and change only
# the ones a specific deployment's real traffic shape calls for.

# Per-(source-IP, providerKey) budget on DLR callbacks. Default derived
# from §4.6's documented "Orange's hard 5 TPS cap" on outbound
# submissions — 10/second leaves 2x headroom for a message receiving more
# than one status callback.
# RATE_LIMIT_DLR_PER_KEY_EVENTS=100
# RATE_LIMIT_DLR_PER_KEY_WINDOW=10s

# Aggregate DLR budget across every (IP, providerKey) pair. 4x the
# per-source ceiling — generous margin since only one provider
# (orange_cm) is wired up this milestone (AGENTS.md).
# RATE_LIMIT_DLR_GLOBAL_EVENTS=200
# RATE_LIMIT_DLR_GLOBAL_WINDOW=10s
